  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「スズメノコビエ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%B9%E3%82%BA%E3%83%A1%E3%83%8E%E3%82%B3%E3%83%93%E3%82%A8",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 106364736,
      "text": "スズメノコビエ Paspalum scrobiculatum L. は、イネ科植物の1種である。日本では普通に見られる野草として扱われるのに対し、インドでは雑穀の1つとして栽培されている。なお、スズメノコビエを雑穀として見た時は、コドやコドミレットやコドラと呼ばれることもある。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Paspalum scrobiculatum」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paspalum_scrobiculatum",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1377201405,
      "text": "Paspalum scrobiculatum, commonly called kodo millet or koda millet, is an annual grain that is grown primarily in Nepal and also in India, Philippines, Indonesia, Vietnam, Thailand, and in West Africa from where it originated. It is grown as a minor crop in most of these areas, with the exception of the Deccan plateau in India where it is grown as a major food source. It is a very hardy crop that is drought tolerant and can survive on marginal soils where other crops may not survive, and can supply 450–900 kg of grain per hectare. Kodo millet has large potential to provide nourishing food to subsistence farmers in Africa and elsewhere."
    }
  },
